  "prediction": "**Department of Defense's Efforts to Prevent and Respond to Unwanted Sexual Behaviors**\n\nThe Department of Defense (DOD) has defined various types of unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault, sexual harassment, and domestic violence. The DOD has established policies and procedures to prevent and respond to these behaviors, but there are areas for improvement.\n\n**Policies and Procedures**\n\nThe DOD has policies in place to prevent and respond to unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault, sexual harassment, and domestic violence. The Under Secretary of Defense for Personnel and Readiness is responsible for developing the overall policy and guidance for the department's efforts to prevent and respond to these behaviors. The Sexual Assault Prevention and Response Office (SAPRO) serves as the department's single point of authority, accountability, and oversight for its sexual assault prevention and response program.\n\n**Gaps in Policies and Procedures**\n\nDespite the existence of policies and procedures, there are gaps in the department's efforts to prevent and respond to unwanted sexual behaviors. The DOD's sexual harassment policies do not consistently include mechanisms for anonymous reporting, as required by the National Defense Authorization Act for FY 2013. Additionally, the department's policies do not fully include principles from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention's (CDC) framework for preventing sexual violence, which includes risk factors, protective factors, primary strategies, secondary strategies, and tertiary strategies.\n\n**Data Collection and Reporting**\n\nThe DOD has processes in place for collecting and reporting data on unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault, sexual harassment, and domestic violence. However, there are inconsistencies in the data collection and reporting processes, which can make it difficult to develop department-wide reports on these behaviors.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) recommends that the DOD:\n\n1. Fully include principles from the CDC's framework for preventing sexual violence in its new policy on sexual harassment.\n2. Include mechanisms for anonymous reporting in its new policy on sexual harassment.\n3. Develop standard data elements and definitions for collecting and reporting data on sexual harassment incidents.\n4. Incorporate a long-term, results-oriented strategic planning framework into its continuum of harm prevention strategy.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe DOD has made progress in establishing policies and procedures to prevent and respond to unwanted sexual behaviors, but there are areas for improvement. The department must continue to work to address the gaps in its policies and procedures, improve its data collection and reporting processes, and develop a comprehensive strategy to prevent and respond to these behaviors.",
